+\section{Generalized critical values and ODE transport}\label{sec:transport}
+We now supply the global transport needed to continue the residue cycle from a
+large parameter to a punctured interval approaching zero.  The point is to do
+this directly on the affine torus, without compactification, Whitney
+stratifications, or Thom isotopy.
 
-\begin{standard}[Complex algebraic stratification and first isotopy]\label{std:whitney}
-A complex algebraic variety, together with finitely many specified
-algebraic subsets, admits a finite complex algebraic Whitney
-stratification compatible with those subsets. If a map from a closed
-Whitney-stratified subset of a smooth manifold to a smooth base is proper
-and restricts to a submersion on every stratum, it is locally topologically
-trivial by homeomorphisms preserving the strata.
+\subsection{A proper metric on the affine torus}
+Write
+\[
+ \mathcal T_d=\{(z,w)\in\C^{2d}:z_jw_j=1\ (1\le j\le d)\}.
+\]
+The map
+\[
+ \iota:\torus\longrightarrow\mathcal T_d,\qquad
+ x\longmapsto(x_1,\ldots,x_d,x_1^{-1},\ldots,x_d^{-1})
+\]
+is an algebraic isomorphism onto the closed smooth affine variety
+$\mathcal T_d$.  Replace every negative power $x_j^{-m}$ in $f$ by $w_j^m$;
+this gives a polynomial $F$ on $\C^{2d}$ whose restriction to
+$\mathcal T_d$ corresponds to $f$.
+
+Equip $\mathcal T_d$ with the metric induced by the Euclidean Hermitian
+metric of $\C^{2d}$, and put
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:proper-radius}
+ \rho(p)=\|p\|,\qquad p\in\mathcal T_d.
+\end{equation}
+Since $\mathcal T_d$ is closed, $\rho$ is proper.  In the original torus
+coordinates,
+\[
+ \rho(x)^2=\sum_{j=1}^d\bigl(|x_j|^2+|x_j|^{-2}\bigr).
+\]
+Thus approaching a coordinate hyperplane $x_j=0$ is correctly counted as
+escape to infinity.
+
+For $p\in\mathcal T_d$, let
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:rabier-function}
+ \lambda(p)=\inf_{|u|=1}\|\dd F_p^*u\|,
+\end{equation}
+where $\dd F_p:T_p\mathcal T_d\to\C$ is regarded as a real linear map and
+$\dd F_p^*$ is its metric adjoint.  Because $F|_{\mathcal T_d}$ is
+holomorphic and the metric is Hermitian, its two real singular values agree;
+consequently
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:scalar-rabier}
+ \lambda(p)=\|\dd F_p\|.
+\end{equation}
+In particular $\lambda(p)=0$ exactly at the critical points of $F$.
+
+Define the ordinary and asymptotic critical-value sets by
+\begin{align}
+ K_0(F)&=F\bigl(\Crit(F)\bigr),\label{eq:K0}\\
+ K_\infty(F)&=\left\{c\in\C:\begin{array}{l}
+ \text{there are }p_\nu\in\mathcal T_d\text{ with }\rho(p_\nu)\to\infty,\\
+ F(p_\nu)\to c,\quad \rho(p_\nu)\lambda(p_\nu)\to0
+ \end{array}\right\}.\label{eq:Kinf}
+\end{align}
+We henceforth use these definitions through the fixed algebraic
+identification with $\torus$; the chosen radius is proper on that torus.
+
+\begin{standard}[Semialgebraic control at infinity]\label{std:asymptotic}
+For a fixed polynomial function on a closed smooth affine semialgebraic
+submanifold equipped with an induced semialgebraic metric, the following
+hold.
+\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
+\item There is an integer $N\ge1$ such that an asymptotic critical value can
+be detected along a sequence satisfying
+\[
+ \lambda(p)\le \rho(p)^{-(1+1/N)}.
+\]
+Equivalently, if
+\[
+ \widetilde\Sigma_R=
+ \{p:\rho(p)\ge R,\ \lambda(p)\le\rho(p)^{-(1+1/N)}\},
+\]
+then
+\begin{equation}\label{eq:Kinf-tail}
+ K_\infty(F)=\bigcap_{R>0}\overline{F(\widetilde\Sigma_R)}.
+\end{equation}
+\item For a fixed semialgebraic family of subsets of Euclidean spheres,
+there is $M<\infty$ such that any two points in one connected component of
+a radius-$r$ member can be joined inside that component by a piecewise
+$C^1$ curve of length at most $Mr$, for all sufficiently large $r$.
+\end{enumerate}
 \end{standard}
 
